      <h1 class="abs-h1">Apple Body Shape</h1>
      <p class="abs-subtitle">Bust and hips close in size, but the waist doesn't taper much between them — fullness sits through the midsection. Roughly 14% of women measure this way. Here's the exact formula, what it means for health, and how to dress for it.</p>
    </header>
  </div>

  <div class="abs-art">

    <div style="text-align:center;margin:0 0 8px;">
      <img src="/assets/images/Apple-Body-Shape-Formula-Measurements-How-to-Dress-300x200.webp" alt="Apple body shape formula, measurements and how to dress" style="max-width:420px;width:100%;border-radius:16px;border:1px solid var(--abs-border);" loading="lazy">
    </div>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-what">
      <h2>What Is an Apple Body Shape?</h2>
      <p>An apple body shape has a bust and hips that are roughly the same width, with a waist that stays close to those measurements rather than tapering inward. The fullness that would normally sit at the hips or bust in other shapes shows up around the midsection instead — belly, ribs and sometimes the upper back. Haven't measured yet? Our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> will confirm which of the seven shapes actually fits your numbers.</p>
      <p>The formula: bust and hips need to be within 3.6 inches of each other, and your waist needs to sit within 2 inches of — or larger than — either the bust or the hips. That second part is what separates apple from rectangle. Both shapes have balanced bust and hips; the difference is entirely in how much waist definition is left.</p>
      <div class="abs-note">
        <p><strong>Quick check:</strong> bust and hips within 3.6 inches of each other, waist within 2 inches of (or bigger than) both of them. If your waist is clearly smaller than either one — by more than 2 inches, just not by a dramatic amount — you're more likely a rectangle.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Want to see the shape rather than just read the formula? Open the <a href="/body-visualizer/">body visualizer</a>, choose the female model and tap the <strong>Apple</strong> chip under <em>Try a shape</em> to rotate an apple silhouette from the front, side and back.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-rare">
      <h2>How Common Is the Apple Shape?</h2>
      <p>The most widely cited figures, from a 2006 NC State analysis of over 6,000 women, put the apple shape at roughly 14% — less common than rectangle (~46%) and pear (~20%), but noticeably more common than the hourglass family (~8% combined). Those specific numbers come from a press release rather than a peer-reviewed table, and later re-analyses have found different splits, so treat them as commonly cited rather than exact. Apple is also one of the shapes most misunderstood in casual conversation, often conflated with body size rather than proportion.</p>
      <p><strong>Apple shape is not a weight category.</strong> It describes where your frame carries fullness relative to your own bust and hip measurements — a slim person can be apple-shaped, and a larger person can be a rectangle or hourglass. Two people at very different sizes can both be genuine apples if their waist-to-bust-to-hip relationship matches the formula.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-compare">
      <h2>Apple vs Rectangle — What's the Real Difference?</h2>
      <p>These two get mixed up constantly, because both have a balanced bust and hip measurement. The waist is the entire distinction.</p>
      <div class="abs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trait</th><th scope="col">Apple</th><th scope="col">Rectangle</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bust vs. hips</td><td>Within 3.6 in of each other</td><td>Within 3.6 in of each other</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waist</td><td>Within 2 in of, or larger than, bust/hips</td><td>More than 2 in smaller, but under 9–10 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visual read</td><td>Fullness sits through the middle</td><td>Straight, uniform column top to bottom</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Styling focus</td><td>Elongate the torso, imply a waistline</td><td>Add curves and definition the frame doesn't naturally have</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>If style advice written for "straight" or "athletic" body types has never quite worked for you, this is often why — rectangle tips assume there's a waist to belt or cinch, and an apple shape doesn't have the same room to work with in that specific spot.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-whr">
      <h2>What Does an Apple Shape Mean for Waist-to-Hip and Waist-to-Height Ratio?</h2>
      <p>Because the waist sits close to (or larger than) the bust and hips, apple shapes tend to run on the higher side of waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) — often somewhere around 0.85 and up for women, at or above the WHO's threshold for elevated cardiovascular and metabolic risk. This is the shape most directly linked to that particular health conversation, because abdominal fat behaves differently, metabolically, from the fat carried in the hips and thighs of a pear shape.</p>
      <p>Waist-to-height ratio (WHtR) is worth checking alongside WHR here specifically, since it doesn't depend on your hip measurement at all — just waist and height. A ratio under 0.5 is the widely cited healthy target. Run both numbers on our <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist to hip ratio calculator</a> and <a href="/waist-to-height-ratio-calculator/">waist to height ratio calculator</a> rather than assuming from shape category alone. The <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a> shows both ratios together with your bust-to-hip ratio.</p>
      <div class="abs-note">
        <p>None of this is a diagnosis. WHR and WHtR are population-level risk indicators, not individual verdicts — genetics, activity level, diet quality, sleep and stress all matter more than any single ratio. If a number concerns you, the right next step is a conversation with a doctor, not a plan built from a calculator result.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-style">
      <h2>How to Dress for an Apple Body Shape</h2>
      <p>The goal is to create the impression of a waistline and elongate the torso — without anything clinging tightly across the midsection, which draws attention exactly where you don't want it.</p>

      <h3>Work and Formal</h3>
      <p>A structured blazer left open over a V-neck top does two things at once: the open front creates a long vertical line, and the V draws the eye down and away from a horizontal read across the middle. Dark, flat-front trousers with a straight or slightly wide leg keep the lower half clean. Avoid pencil skirts with a fitted waistband that sits directly on the fullest part.</p>

      <h3>Casual and Everyday</h3>
      <p>A wrap top or tunic over leggings or straight trousers is close to a uniform for this shape, and for good reason — the wrap creates a diagonal line that reads as a waist without any actual compression. Vertical stripes, seams or panel details in prints do a similar job. Skip anything boxy and shapeless; it removes the illusion of a waistline entirely rather than creating one.</p>

      <h3>Special Occasions</h3>
      <p>An empire-waist gown, cut just under the bust, is built specifically for this shape — everything below flows freely, and the visual waistline sits in a spot that's actually defined. Flowing chiffon or crepe in a single vertical panel from bust to hem elongates the whole silhouette.</p>

      <h3>Swimwear</h3>
      <p>A one-piece with ruching or a wrap-front panel across the midsection is the most reliable choice — it adds texture and a diagonal line exactly where a plain fabric would just sit flat. A high-waisted bottom paired with a structured top works for two-piece styles. Avoid a plain, low-rise bikini bottom with a triangle top; there's nothing in that combination creating a waist illusion.</p>

      <h3>What to Avoid</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Wide belts cinched at the natural waist — they sit directly on the fullest point and emphasise it rather than defining it</li>
        <li>Clingy, stretchy fabric with no structure across the midsection</li>
        <li>Low-rise bottoms, which cut across the waist at its widest point instead of above or below it</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-measure">
      <h2>Common Apple Shape Measurements by Size</h2>
      <p>The pattern holds at every size — bust and hips close together, waist close behind or level with them:</p>
      <div class="abs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Frame</th><th scope="col">Bust</th><th scope="col">Waist</th><th scope="col">Hips</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Petite</td><td>34 in</td><td>34 in</td><td>36 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Average</td><td>38 in</td><td>38 in</td><td>40 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Plus-size</td><td>46 in</td><td>46 in</td><td>48 in</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>In every row, the waist is within an inch or two of the bust — there's no 9-inch taper anywhere in sight. That's the entire signature of this shape, regardless of overall size.</p>
      <p>Try the average row in the <a href="/body-visualizer/">Body Visualizer</a>: switch to inches, enter a 38 in bust, 38 in waist and 40 in hips, and the live analysis confirms the shape while you rotate the model.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="abs-sec" id="abs-faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details class="abs-faq"><summary>What is the exact formula for an apple body shape?</summary><div class="abs-faq-body">
        <p>Bust and hips within 3.6 inches of each other, and a waist within 2 inches of — or equal to or larger than — both the bust and the hips. Both parts matter: the first rules out pear and inverted triangle, and the second is what separates apple from rectangle, which has the same balanced bust and hips but a bit more waist definition.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="abs-faq"><summary>Is an apple body shape unhealthy?</summary><div class="abs-faq-body">
        <p>Shape itself isn't a diagnosis. What the research does show is that abdominal fat — the kind associated with an apple shape — is more metabolically active than fat stored in the hips and thighs, and is linked to higher cardiovascular and metabolic risk at a population level. Waist-to-hip ratio and waist-to-height ratio are the specific numbers worth tracking if this is a concern, not the shape label on its own.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="abs-faq"><summary>What's the difference between apple and rectangle?</summary><div class="abs-faq-body">
        <p>Both have a bust and hip measurement that are close together. The waist is the difference: a rectangle's waist is more than 2 inches smaller than the bust and hips (just not dramatically so), while an apple's waist sits within 2 inches of them, or even matches or exceeds them.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="abs-faq"><summary>Does an apple shape mean I'm overweight?</summary><div class="abs-faq-body">
        <p>No. Apple describes a proportion — the relationship between your bust, waist and hip measurements — not a weight category. A slim person can be apple-shaped just as easily as a larger person can be a rectangle or hourglass. It's about where your particular frame carries its fullness, not how much of it there is.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="abs-faq"><summary>Can pregnancy or menopause cause an apple shape?</summary><div class="abs-faq-body">
        <p>Yes, both are common triggers for a shift toward this shape. Pregnancy changes waist measurements, sometimes permanently. Menopause-related hormonal shifts often move fat storage from the hips toward the abdomen, which is why someone who measured as a pear or hourglass earlier in life may measure as an apple later on. Re-measuring every six months, or after a major life change, keeps your result current.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="abs-faq"><summary>What kind of exercise suits an apple shape?</summary><div class="abs-faq-body">
        <p>There's no exercise that changes your underlying frame or converts one shape into another, but general strength training and regular movement support a healthier waist-to-hip and waist-to-height ratio over time, alongside diet and sleep. If you're starting a new programme, a trainer or doctor familiar with your specific situation is a better source of a plan than a shape calculator.</p>
      </div></details>
    </section>

    <p>The apple label carries more baggage than any of the other six shapes, mostly because it gets tangled up with conversations about weight that have nothing to do with proportion. Once you separate the two — a formula about bust, waist and hip relationships on one side, and body size on the other — the shape itself is just one more way a frame can be built, with its own specific things that fit well.</p>

